--TEST--
`Core\Session::setShortLived` and `Core\Session::setLongLived` encode their value into a record a store outlives the request holding, so a `secret` is refused there with the same code every other carrier reports — and the help names the sealed sibling of each, `setShortLivedSecret` and `setLongLivedSecret`, because a user's own secret has a sealed door of its own in the session — `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary`, `rule:http-server/a-session-holds-a-secret-only-sealed`, `rule:http-server/a-session-value-is-short-lived-or-long-lived`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary` refuses a `secret` at the one
// recursive graph copy, and each write makes one: the record it changes is
// encoded to bytes a store holds until the session ends, and nothing on the far
// side of that copy carries the qualifier. The sealed door is the only door, and
// the diagnostic says which one it is rather than offering a reveal that would
// put a plaintext credential in a session store.
class Vault {
    public static function token(): secret string {
        return "sk-live";
    }
}

string $plain = "plain";
secret string $s = "s";

// The accepted side first, so this is a bound with both sides named.
Core\Session::setShortLived("cart", $plain);
Core\Session::setLongLived("theme", $plain);

// Written positionally.
Core\Session::setShortLived("refresh", $s);

// Filled by name, which reaches the same parameter — the refusal is read off
// the argument's slot rather than off its position.
Core\Session::setShortLived(key: "refresh", value: $s);

// Composed rather than declared: the qualifier poisons through concatenation,
// so a value assembled at the call site reaches the same refusal.
Core\Session::setShortLived("refresh", "bearer " . $s);

// Declared one frame away, which is where a rule that only looked at variables
// would miss it.
Core\Session::setShortLived("refresh", Vault::token());

// The long lifetime takes the same rule, and its help names its own sealed sibling.
Core\Session::setLongLived("device", $s);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::setShortLived` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
   = help: write `setShortLivedSecret($key, $value, $keys)` instead. It encrypts the value before it is stored. Or reveal it first with `Core\Secret::reveal(..., "reason")`, at the call where the secret value is needed
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::setShortLived` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::setShortLived` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::setShortLived` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::setLongLived` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
   = help: write `setLongLivedSecret($key, $value, $keys)` instead. It encrypts the value before it is stored. Or reveal it first with `Core\Secret::reveal(..., "reason")`, at the call where the secret value is needed
%A
